### Step 1: Identify product R and its molecular weight

The molecular weight of R (1,4-dibromomethylbenzene, \(C_{7}H_{6}Br_{2}\)) is:
M.W. = (7 \times 12) + (6 \times 1) + (2 \times 80) = 84 + 6 + 160 = 250 \text{ g/mol}

### Step 2: Calculate moles of R

1 \text{ g R} \rightarrow \frac{1}{250} \text{ moles}

### Step 3: Determine moles of Br atoms in R

Since R has 2 bromine atoms per molecule:
\text{No. of Br Atoms} \rightarrow \frac{2}{250} \text{ moles}

### Step 4: Calculate moles of AgBr formed

In Carius method, each bromine atom from the organic compound forms one molecule of AgBr. Therefore, moles of AgBr formed will be equal to the moles of Br atoms.
\text{Moles of AgBr} \rightarrow \frac{2}{250} \text{ moles}

### Step 5: Calculate the mass of AgBr

Molecular weight of AgBr = Atomic mass of Ag + Atomic mass of Br = \(108 + 80 = 188\) g/mol
\text{Mass of AgBr} = \frac{2}{250} \times (108+80) = \frac{2}{250} \times 188 = 1.504 \text{ g}
The amount of AgBr formed is 1.504 g.

